cartridge "CROSSROADS II/S.HARTER/1988"

' CROSSROADS II: PANDEMONIUM
' A ColecoVision game based on "Crossroads II: Pandemonium" by Steve
' Harter, COMPUTE!'s Gazette December 1988 (Commodore 64 machine language,
' SYS2445; also on the Gazette's Best of 1988 disk with its article).
' Made for Amy Studio by Claude (an AI made by Anthropic), 2026, in Amy by
' Amy Bienvenu.
'
' Not a line-by-line port: the rules are the original's (its article,
' orig/article.txt, play in jsc64 and its machine code, orig/xroads2.dis),
' the game is built for the ColecoVision: tube mazes in a new colour for
' every maze, little 8 x 8 creatures that glide from cell to cell, sound
' effects and an original title tune.
'
' The game: one or two players (at the same time) run the corridors of a
' maze, shoot creatures and pick up the whirling spars. A spar is worth
' 500 points and a shield; a shield stops one hit; a hit without a shield
' costs a life. When the players together hold five spars the next maze
' comes. Red spars give quicker, more numerous shots, blue spars double
' the speed; both are lost with a life. Creatures pick up spars too (each
' one a shield), and whatever dies drops its spars at random in the maze.
' Creatures arrive five at a time and more come during the level; every
' 30 seconds they get faster. Long open corridors wrap round, and so do
' the shots that run down them.
'
' From the original (orig/): the eight mazes, bit for bit (8 x 55 bytes at
' $2006: 22 rows of 20 cells, the right half mirrored, the bottom row the
' top one again; the corridors and the edges wrap); a level ends when the
' two players' spar counts ($5000 + $5001) reach 5 (code at $185D);
' four lives and three shields at the start; 500 points a spar. All 16
' creature types, from the type tables at $1F48-$1FE2: the first level
' each comes ($1F48: a random type is drawn again until its level has come,
' as at $118A), its shields ($1F6C), its move delay ($1F80: the player's
' is 2, so a creature moves at 2/delay of the player's speed; timed in
' jsc64, orig/timing*.mjs) and its colour ($1FBA). What each does follows
' its projectile byte ($1F94: none = it rams) and the article: shooters
' (light blue drone, the yellow Lemonshark, red imp, blue crab, the light
' red creature and the supercreature), rammers (brown mole, grey robot,
' dark grey tank, orange spider; their shields are not used up when they
' ram), the purple and light green rubberheads (a shot in the back comes
' back; they charge a player they see), the white creature (eats spars,
' faster for each), the Green Vacuum (sucks up anything despite shields,
' from level 7), the small Brown Dog (hunts the creatures, cannot hurt you;
' above level 19 five shields and the player's speed, as at $11F1), the
' light red human mutant (from level 2) that jumps onto the light red
' creature (from level 8) to make the supercreature (also alone from level
' 17). Creatures alone turn invisible (from level 3); faster every 30
' seconds (a step off every delay).
'
' Liberties: cells are 5 x 7 pixels (the C64's 8 x 8 do not fit 39 cells
' across), drawn as tubes. The player runs a cell in 8 frames (blue: 4);
' creatures keep the original's delay ratios. Points for a creature are
' its shields x 100 (the C64's values were not found). The names of the
' kinds the article does not name are made up. The game runs 30 passes a
' second; the creatures move in two halves (each half every other pass)
' and at most one creature chooses its way per pass, so the frame budget
' holds with nine creatures. Difficulty ramp (the original's levels were
' not measured): creatures alive at once 4 to 9 and the wait between
' arrivals by level (LvMax, LvCome), the first crowd a creature every 4
' passes (from level 5 the whole crowd), a player holds at most 5 shields, the speed-ups start further on, gunners are keener to fire and
' their shots quicker at higher levels. Shots don't hurt the other player;
' a creature's shot passes its own kind; after a life is lost the player
' is unseen and safe for 2 seconds; an invisible creature glints briefly
' three times a second at most, so a first-time player has a fair hint. No
' pause and no colour choice (they used the C64's function keys); the
' level (1-9) and the number of players are chosen with the stick on the
' title. The demo mode is replaced by the title page. The C64 asked to
' play again; here GAME OVER returns to the title.
'
' Controls: controller 1 for player 1, controller 2 for player 2 (in a
' one-player game either one): the stick runs, either button shoots
' (hold it for a stream). On the title: up and down choose a line, left and
' right change it, a button starts.
'
' House rules: text in columns 2-29, rows 1-22; tiles from code 128; the
' frame loop is protected from the NMI; screens drawn with the NMI off
' after a wait, display off. Music first in the sound table, effects after;
' the title tune is played once. `set sprite count` is always given. Own
' xorshift random numbers.

const TShield = 128
